osity, is it *really* the same thing? Technically, no. One is a pre-encoded, static file sitting on a server (like YouTube). The other, cloud gaming, is a dynamically generated, on-the-fly encoded stream. These are fundamentally different video streams, even if the pixels end up looking identical on the screen. It's like the pizza analogy: you can order a pizza and have it delivered or have a private chef prepare the pizza in your kitchen with premium ingredients. The taste of the final product may be identical, but the logistics of the two are completely different. I'm thinking, a great analogy might be water pipes. You get a glass of clear water. Does it really matter if it came from a slow drip filter or a firehose? Probably not! The experience is the same. But your water bill certainly cares. The point of cloud gaming is to make the plumbing invisible, and, for many, it will be, but there are instances where the user will be reminded of the logistics behind this "invisible" plumbing.
